,"miss","mistake","best","mistake","miss","best","best","best","best","best","good","inaccuracy","best","best","best","good","best","good","best","inaccuracy","best","blunder","best"],"moments":[{"ply":60,"name":"30…Nh6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"30…Nh6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moving away uncovers the bishop on g6, which now hits the rook on d3.","Attacks the g4 pawn, which is undefended.","The next best move, 30…Nd6, would drop Black's winning chances from 65% to 51% (−0.16).","30…Nh6 gets the knight out of the way of gxf5.","White was threatening gxf5, winning a knight for a pawn. The knight steps away.","White answers 31.Kf3."]},{"ply":61,"name":"31.Rc3","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"31.Rc3 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 36% to 22%.","points":["Black answers 31…Nxg4+, which takes the pawn on g4.","After Nxg4+, Black wins 2 points of material within 7 moves.","Better was 31.Kf3 (−1.55), which protects the g4 pawn, which was hanging."],"better":"31.Kf3 protects the g4 pawn. After 31…Be4+ 32.Kf4 Bxd3 33.Bxd3 f6 34.Bf5, White is two pawns down (−1.55)."},{"ply":64,"name":"32…Nf6","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"32…Nf6 lets the win slip: 32…Bh5 kept Black winning. Black's winning chances go from 78% to 58%.","points":["White answers 33.Bd3.","Better was 32…Bh5 (−3.39): the bishop lines up with the White king, behind the knight on g4."],"better":"32…Bh5 stops Kxg4. After 33.e4 Nf6+ 34.Kf4 dxe4 35.Bc4 Bg6, Black comes out a pawn up (−3.39)."},{"ply":65,"name":"33.g4","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"33.g4 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 42% to 30%.","points":["Black answers 33…Rh8: the rook takes the open h-file.","Better was 33.Bd3 (−0.83)."],"better":"33.Bd3 prepares Ke2 and Kd2. After 33…Be4+ 34.Ke2 Rh8 35.Kd2 Rh2 36.Rg1 Bxd3 37.Kxd3 Ng4, the bishops are traded (−0.83)."},{"ply":67,"name":"34.Kf4","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"34.Kf4 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 29% to 18%.","points":["Black answers 34…b6, which attacks the knight on c5.","Better was 34.Bd3 (−2.47), which takes c2, b1 away from the bishop on g6, which is left with 3 usable squares."],"better":"34.Bd3 restricts the bishop on g6. After 34…Bxd3 35.Nxd3 Ne4 36.Rc2 Rh3+ 37.Kg2, the bishops are traded (−2.47)."},{"ply":68,"name":"34…Rh2","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"34…Rh2 misses the chance 34.Kf4 gave: 34…b6 would have punished it. Black's winning chances go from 82% to 71%.","points":["White answers 35.Re2, which protects the b2 pawn, which was hanging.","Better was 34…b6 (−4.05), which attacks the knight on c5."],"better":"34…b6 threatens …bxc5, winning a knight. After 35.Nxa6 Re4+ 36.Kf3 Rxg4 37.Bd3 Bxd3 38.Rxd3 Rh3+, the bishops are traded (−4.05)."},{"ply":85,"name":"43.Bxa6","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"43.Bxa6 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 0% to 0%. It allows mate in 2.","points":["Black answers 43…Rh2+: the rook reaches the seventh rank, where 1 pawn sits.","Better was 43.Be2 (−18.29): moving away uncovers the rook on c3, which now hits the rook on e3."],"better":"43.Be2 uncovers an attack on the rook on e3. After 43…Rh2+ 44.Kf1 Rexe2 45.Rxg4+ Kxg4 46.b3, White is the exchange plus a pawn down (−18.29)."}],"opening":{"name":"Queen's Gambit Declined: Ragozin Defense","eco":"D38","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 11…Bd6: 22 moves in all, 11 by White and 11 by Black.","Theory runs out after 11…Bd6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}